Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListCount || New_Ret == Old_arrListCount - 1);
</code>

This assertion checks if the new count after executing an Add operation is greater than or equal to the old count. The new count may be equal to the old count, in case that the ArrayList is already full and cannot add any more elements.

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListCount - 1);
</code>

This assertion checks if the new count after executing a Remove operation is less than the old count. In other words, an element has been removed, so the size of the ArrayList must have decreased by exactly 1.

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_Ret <= Old_arrListCount + 1);
</code>

This assertion checks if the new index after executing an Insert operation is in the valid range for an ArrayList. The new index should be greater than or equal to 0 (since ArrayList indices start from 0) and less than or equal to the old count plus 1 (since you can insert at index count, which is valid).

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListCount);
</code>

This assertion checks if the new count remains the same after executing a Set operation. This indicates that the array list has not grown or shrunk, only the elements within it have been modified.

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);
</code>

This assertion checks if the new count after executing a Get operation is the same as the old count. There has been no change to the size of the ArrayList.

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListCount || New_Ret == Old_arrListCount - 1);
</code>

This assertion checks if the new count after executing an Add operation is greater than or equal to the old count. The new count may be equal to the old count, in case that the ArrayList is already full and contains no elements.

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListCount - 1);
</code>

This assertion checks if the new count after executing a Remove operation is less than the old count. In other words, an element has been removed, so the size of the ArrayList must have decreased by exactly 1.

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListCount);
</code>

This assertion checks if the new count after executing a Count operation is the same as the old count. The size of the array list has not changed.

Remember these assertions are just examples and should be adapted to fit your specific needs.
